---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="About" current="about" description="What the forecasts on this site show and where they come from.">
  <h1>About these forecasts</h1>

  <div class="stack" style="margin-top:20px">
    <section class="card">
      <h2>This catalog</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Windgrams lists paragliding launches. Coordinates and the direction each launch faces come from the flyingsites
        catalog. A local <code>pnpm dev</code> serves whatever forecast documents are in <code>forecasts/data</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>Live at the launch</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Where a personal weather station stands at or next to a launch, the top of the launch page can show what it measures
        now: wind direction, speed and gusts compared with the launch's own limits, temperature, humidity, pressure and
        sunshine, and the last 12 hours as a chart. This published site does not poll stations, so that panel is not filled in.
        A station reading is a measurement near the launch, not on it: always check the wind on launch. Station data:
        <a href="https://www.wunderground.com/">Weather Underground</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>Soaring meteogram</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Weather models forecast the whole atmosphere above each launch. The meteogram shows, hour by hour, how high thermals
        should reach, where the cloud base is, how stable the air is, and the wind at every height. It is built with
        <a href="https://meteo.azohra.com/">meteo by Azohra</a> from two national weather services. The site is published
        twice a day, about 06:15 and 18:15 UTC, from the latest 00 and 12 UTC runs. A 06 or 18 UTC run waits for the next publish.
      </p>
      <ul style="margin-top:8px">
        <li><strong>Today and tomorrow</strong>: NOAA's HRRR (3 km) and Canada's HRDPS (2.5 km). When they agree, trust the day more.</li>
        <li><strong>Out to day 3</strong>: NOAA's RRFS (3 km) and Canada's RDPS (10 km).</li>
        <li><strong>Long range</strong>: NOAA's GFS (25 km, 16 days) and Canada's GDPS (15 km, 10 days) for the trend.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ensemble (GEPS)</strong>: Canada's model run 21 times with slightly different starts. The shaded bands show the
          spread: wide bands mean the long range is uncertain.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        A 3 km model sees a smoothed mountain, so its ground is often lower than the real launch. The launch elevation line
        on the chart is measured from terrain data, not taken from the model.
        <a href="https://meteo.azohra.com/docs/briefing/reading-a-meteogram/">Learn to read every mark on the chart</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>Sounding (Skew-T)</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        A Skew-T is the chart forecasters use to judge the air above a place: temperature and moisture at every height, and how a
        thermal would rise through them. This one is drawn from the selected model for any hour of the day and marked up for
        pilots: the thermal zone, the top of the lift, cloud base, inversions that cap the thermals, cloud layers and the wind. The
        panel beside it says the same in plain words. Its numbers are the same ones the soaring meteogram uses.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>Weather balloon</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Twice a day the National Weather Service launches weather balloons (00 and 12 UTC). The forecast-discussion section
        draws the latest flights from the upper-air station nearest the launch as a Skew-T. The morning flight is read the way the NWS Soaring
        Forecast is made: a thermal leaves the ground at the afternoon forecast high and rises until it is no warmer than the
        morning air, which gives the day's thermal top. The Soaring Forecast's own numbers (trigger temperature, lift, soaring
        index) are shown alongside, and its model hours can be viewed as Skew-Ts too. The twice-daily publish picks up
        whichever of those flights is out. Balloon data: NWS, via the
        <a href="https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/archive/raob/">Iowa Environmental Mesonet</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>National Weather Service forecast</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        The official forecast issued by the National Weather Service for the launch's 2.5 km grid cell — the same data as the
        hourly graph on <a href="https://www.weather.gov/">weather.gov</a>. It is refreshed twice a day, with the rest of the site.
      </p>
      <ul style="margin-top:8px">
        <li><strong>Mixing height</strong>: how deep the air near the ground is being stirred; a rough ceiling for thermals.</li>
        <li><strong>Transport wind</strong>: the average wind through that mixed layer; what you will feel once you climb out.</li>
        <li><strong>Launch window arrows</strong>: green when the surface wind blows from the directions the launch works in.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>ECMWF forecast</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        The European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts' IFS model, often the best global model, at 9 km: surface wind
        and gusts at the launch, winds at about 5,000 and 10,000 ft, how deep the boundary layer mixes, and low, mid and high
        cloud, hour by hour for a week. It is a second opinion next to the National Weather Service forecast. Its open data has
        no surface heat flux, so it cannot drive the soaring meteogram. Data © ECMWF (CC BY 4.0), served by
        <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo.com</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>Fly safe</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Forecasts are model output and forecaster judgment, not observations. They can be wrong about timing, wind and
        storms. Always check real conditions at launch, respect local site rules, and fly within your own limits.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
